[FINAL-TEXT] Confirmed. Neither router3 nor router5 has a route to 192.168.200.0/24 — the pc11–pc14 LAN. Root cause is clear.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router4
diagnosis_text:
Router4's FRR RIP configuration is missing the `network 192.168.200.0/24` statement. Its running config contains only two `network` entries: `172.25.0.0/16` (covering the eth5 WAN link to router3 at 172.25.1.0/24) and `192.168.15.0/24` (covering the eth4 WAN link to router5). The directly-connected LAN subnet 192.168.200.0/24 — where pc11, pc12, pc13, and pc14 reside — is never announced into the RIP domain.

Because no RIP advertisement for 192.168.200.0/24 is ever sent, every other router in the topology (router3, router5, and transitively all upstream routers) has zero knowledge of this prefix. Verified on both router3 and router5 routing tables — 192.168.200.0/24 is absent from both. Outbound packets from pc11–pc14 reach other sites normally (router4 has a kernel default route), but all return traffic is blackholed at whatever upstream router is closest: that router has no route to 192.168.200.0/24 and sends back ICMP unreachable, making the gateway (192.168.200.1) appear dead to anyone outside the segment. Users inside the LAN can still ping 192.168.200.1 locally, but every remote host and every remote site is unreachable — matching the ticket's "whole site is stuck, gateway answers nobody."

Fix: on router4, enter `vtysh`, then under `router rip` add `network 192.168.200.0/24`. Router4 will immediately begin advertising that prefix, and within one RIP update cycle (≤30 s) all other routers will install the route and the site will regain full connectivity.
=== END ===